Scope read: internal/services/storage/storage.go (fileSync/syncDir seams,
createExclusive, atomicWrite), auth.go (saveConfig), durability_test.go (9
new tests). Diffed against f36f438 (pre-T8 HEAD) to isolate exactly what
this attempt changed:
  git diff f36f438 -- internal/services/storage/auth.go internal/services/storage/storage.go
confirms the only behavioral change is: fileSync(f) before Close in all
three paths, syncDir(dir) after Rename/Link in all three paths, both errors
returned (wrapped with %w in saveConfig, preserving errors.Is).

1. Completeness within scope. Grepped the package for other
   os.WriteFile/os.Rename/os.Link/os.CreateTemp call sites:
   `grep -n "os.WriteFile\|os.Rename\|os.Link\|CreateTemp" internal/services/storage/*.go`
   finds two more direct os.WriteFile calls in migration.go (verifyPath,
   markerPath) that bypass the staging regime entirely and are therefore not
   synced. These predate this branch (migration.go is untouched by T8's
   diff; confirmed via `git status`/`git diff --stat` showing only auth.go,
   storage.go, and the new test file changed) and are explicitly disclosed
   in a pre-existing doc comment at migration.go:44-48 ("written with a bare
   os.WriteFile ... outside the stage-and-rename contract ... not a pattern
   to copy for user data"). The new doc comment on the fileSync/syncDir
   seams (storage.go:509) claims coverage of "every stage-and-publish write
   in this package" — accurate, since migration.go's writes are not
   stage-and-publish (no staging file/rename). No overclaim found. Traced
   Storage.WriteFile -> WriteFileContext -> atomicWrite to confirm the
   user-data path funnels through the fixed function.

2. Error-path holes. Read all three functions end to end. In each: Write
   error closes f and returns (ignoring the close error, consistent with
   pre-existing pattern); fileSync error likewise closes-and-returns,
   dropping only the Close error, never the fileSync error; a successful
   fileSync is followed by a checked Close() whose error is returned before
   Chmod/Rename/Link runs; after a successful Rename/Link, syncDir(dir)'s
   return value is what the function returns (createExclusive's syncDir(dir)
   is the final statement, not "return nil"; atomicWrite and saveConfig
   match). syncDir's own body ("first error among open/Sync/Close wins") is
   correct as read, though see item 3 for a coverage gap in verifying that
   specific internal precedence directly. Confirmed the directory argument
   matches in all three (filepath.Dir(path) for the two storage.go
   functions, baseDir for saveConfig) against what the tests assert.

3. Mutation testing (scratch copy under /tmp/sb_scratch, restored/deleted
   after). Ran real mutants against the actual test suite:
   - Default fileSync mutated to a no-op (`return nil` instead of
     `f.Sync()`): full package suite still passes
     (`go test -count=1 ./internal/services/storage/...` -> ok, 134s). This
     survivor is real but the task's own framing calls it out as
     untestable-in-principle with seam-based tests (verifying an actual
     fsync syscall occurred requires instrumentation outside a Go unit
     test); grounding instead comes from reading the default implementation
     directly, which does call f.Sync() and, in syncDir, opens the real
     directory and calls d.Sync(). Recorded as an observation, not a
     defect.
   - atomicWrite's post-rename syncDir(dir) argument swapped to
     syncDir(filepath.Dir(dir)) (wrong directory): caught —
     TestAtomicWriteSyncsStagingFileAndDirectoryOnSuccess fails
     ("syncDir called with .../NNN, want .../NNN/001").
   - atomicWrite's syncDir error swallowed (`_ = syncDir(dir); return nil`
     instead of `return syncDir(dir)`): caught —
     TestAtomicWriteDirSyncFailurePropagates fails ("atomicWrite error =
     <nil>, want errInjectedSync").
   No planted mutant that represents a genuine, fixable defect survived.

4. Concurrency. No test in the package (not just durability_test.go) calls
   t.Parallel — confirmed by grep across all *_test.go files in the
   package; the one hit is a comment, not a call — so the package-level
   var seams are never mutated concurrently by this suite.
   `go test -race -count=1 -timeout 1800s ./internal/services/storage/...`
   passed clean in 822s (0 "--- FAIL", 0 "DATA RACE", within the task's
   ~1030s budget). Note: a first attempt without an explicit -timeout hit
   Go's default 600s test-binary timeout and was killed mid-scrypt-KDF
   under race instrumentation (encryption tests are CPU-bound and much
   slower under -race) — that is an artifact of my own invocation, not a
   race or product defect; the corrected run with adequate timeout is
